{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head b80502a6259d0f91308b41ba79cfa93b97ab8a57, artifact 0199d530ee33b9010d64a6a72169b6cdd3a19f14. This replaces the report at 68c0f42f; codex raised four findings against that head and all four are closed here.\n\nOne condition remains unmet, and I state it first because I now know why it cannot be met by trying harder.\n\nThe end-to-end dogfood on a real gitseq issue still has not happened. It needs a scoped outbound charter, and I have found that the connector would refuse any charter an operator states. internal/connector/github/read.go:49 rejects a clause that is stale as well as one that is retired, and replacing a charter makes its successor stale by construction — the successor must cite what it replaces, and what it replaces is then retired. Every one of hugh's governance acts for this connector is in exactly that position today: charter 2533 and clauses 2459 and 2666 are all stale and none is retired, so the connector reports no live admission clause while looking at live, ratified, correctly scoped governance. Filed as a child request on this promise rather than worked around here, because it is a change to admission semantics and deserves its own review.\n\nWhat the four findings cost, and what closed them.\n\nThe charter said where the connector may act and not what it may do, so a charter written to authorize reading also authorized writing. It now carries operations, and a charter declaring none authorizes observation alone.\n\nThe propose command required a request, an artifact and a commit, and treated supplied as true. Three well-formed strings naming records with nothing to do with each other would have opened a pull request citing a request that does not govern that artifact, or an artifact naming different code — the case the command's own comment said it must refuse. The relationship is now checked against the projection before the POST, with a separate refusal for an unknown event, a stale one, and one describing other code, because those are different mistakes.\n\nOpen() accepted a created pull request with no usable number or no url, which would have left a promise looking kept with nothing able to close it.\n\nTwo comments overclaimed. The marker does not exclude the connector's own writing from its reading. I closed that by removing the claim rather than by wiring the marker inbound: filtering observations on a string any GitHub user can type would let an author hide an issue by pasting it, turning a label into a denial vector. What actually excludes the outbound half is that the inbound half skips pull requests.\n\nThe head also removes a built binary I committed by accident in the predecessor commit, and ignores the shape that produces it.\n\ngo vet passes, the full suite passes, the worktree is clean at this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0199d530ee33b9010d64a6a72169b6cdd3a19f14","branch":"request/github-connector-render","head":"b80502a6259d0f91308b41ba79cfa93b97ab8a57","status":"ready-for-review"}}